module bindings.ruby.controller_auth_filter_skipped_with_declared_policy;

// A Rails controller that runs an authentication before_action can switch it
// off per action, and `skip_before_action` documents the two scopes for that:
// `only:` names the actions that lose the filter and `except:` names the ones
// that keep it. Called with the filter alone, it switches the filter off for
// every action in the controller. That is a legitimate arrangement when the
// whole controller is public by design -- sign-in and password-reset
// controllers have to be reachable before anyone is authenticated -- and it
// undoes the policy of a controller that declares one.
//
// The declaration side is the same vocabulary the Ruby `core.AuthorizationCheck`
// bindings treat as an access decision: Pundit's `authorize`, CanCanCan's
// `authorize!` and `can?`, and the framework hooks `check_authorization`,
// `action_auth_level`, `check_ownership`, `require_owner` and `owns_resource?`.
// A class that registers an access level per action and then disables the
// filter that consults the registry has a policy on paper only: whatever level
// an action declares, any caller the base controller still authenticates gets
// in. `scope.call` is the confining route for that conjunct -- it reads the
// sibling calls of the same class body, so a controller with no authorization
// declaration of its own does not report however publicly it is configured.
//
// The filter-name needle `authenticate` covers Rails' documented
// `authenticate_or_request_with_*` family, Devise's `authenticate_user!`-style
// filters and application hooks named for authentication; `verify_authenticity_token`
// does not contain it, so the forgery filter keeps its own binding.
//
// Costs and residuals, stated rather than hidden.
//
//   * `args.count == 1` is the unscoped test, so any second argument removes
//     the finding: an `only:`/`except:` scope, and equally Rails' `raise: false`
//     alone. The presence of a scope is the discriminator, not its breadth --
//     a skip scoped by a three-action `except:` over a controller whose other
//     actions also declare levels stays unreported, because those actions keep
//     declaring a policy the filter no longer consults.
//   * A public-by-design controller that authorizes per resource inside its
//     actions -- Pundit's `authorize` called in a method body -- reports too,
//     because the declaration conjunct cannot tell a per-action level from a
//     per-resource check.
//   * The filter name is read from the call's arguments, so an application
//     whose authentication hook is not named for authentication is invisible
//     to the needle.
//
// On the reachable Ruby set: four controllers across one application fire, each
// one declaring per-action authorization levels while skipping the
// authentication filter for the whole class; the 154 controller files of a
// second Rails application and five library checkouts report none.
binding controllerAuthFilterSkippedWithDeclaredPolicy {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.method == "skip_before_action"
    and args.count == 1
    and args.any.literal contains "authenticate"
    and scope.call.method in ["authorize", "authorize!", "can?", "check_authorization", "action_auth_level", "check_ownership", "require_owner", "owns_resource?"]
  emit issue code.AccessPolicyReview at call
}
